  - m_Id: 11185858504810496
    m_Localized: Sogdian
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753536
    m_Localized: Sogdian belongs to the Eastern Iranian branch and is written in
      the Sogdian alphabet - the very script that later gave rise to the Uyghur writing
      system, and through it to Mongolian and Manchu. From the fifth to the eighth
      century it served as the principal commercial language of the Silk Road from
      Dunhuang and Chang'an in the east to Samarkand and Merv in the west. Trade
      letters in Sogdian have been found in caves near Dunhuang; merchant colonies
      in Tang China maintained correspondence with their homeland in this tongue.
      A knowledge of Sogdian opens doors to the most reliable broker networks on
      the road. By the end of the era Arabization displaces it from business use,
      but in the memory of living merchants it still holds.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753537
    m_Localized: Chinese
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753538
    m_Localized: The Chinese of the Tang dynasty is Middle Chinese in its court and
      chancery forms; the logographic script is shared by all educated people regardless
      of spoken dialect. It is used in imperial edicts, Buddhist sutra translations,
      poetry, and diplomatic correspondence. In the port of Guangzhou and in the
      foreign quarters of Chang'an a merchant without Chinese depends on an interpreter
      - which means added expense and the risk of being misled. Knowing the language
      allows direct dealings with officials, reading customs registers, and taking
      part in government supply auctions. Chinese also serves as a bridge language
      for Japanese and Korean traders visiting Tang.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753539
    m_Localized: Tibetan
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753540
    m_Localized: The Tibetan language is written in an alphabet devised in the seventh
      century on the orders of King Songtsen Gampo, modeled on Indian Brahmi - created
      specifically for translating Buddhist canons from Sanskrit. During the height
      of the Tibetan Empire (618-842) a knowledge of Tibetan is essential for trading
      in Lhasa and in the Tarim Basin oases when they are under Tibetan control.
      Tibet's monasteries are repositories of texts and at the same time nodal points
      of mountain commerce, where valley grain is exchanged for plateau salt. After
      842 Tibet's political unity collapses, but the monastic network keeps the language
      alive. For a merchant traveling through Nepal toward India or along mountain
      trails into Yunnan, Tibetan is the key to a passable route.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753541
    m_Localized: Turkic
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753542
    m_Localized: 'Turkic is more precisely a family of closely related tongues spoken
      from Manchuria to Semirechye. Old Turkic writing - the Orkhon-Yenisei runes
      - left inscriptions on stone steles across the Mongolian steppe, recording
      khagan titles and epic narratives. From 744 the Uyghur Khaganate becomes the
      dominant steppe power and adopts the Manichaean faith, lending the language
      new cultural prestige. A knowledge of Turkic opens routes through the steppe
      belt and allows direct negotiation with khagan border guards at the checkpoints.
      The Uyghurs control key nodes of the silk trade between Tang and the west:
      without Turkic there is no bargaining here.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753543
    m_Localized: Sanskrit
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753544
    m_Localized: Sanskrit is the classical literary language of Indian scholarship,
      written in the Tang period in scripts of the Brahmi family, including Gupta
      and Sharada. It is not the spoken tongue of any people, but is understood by
      educated individuals across the subcontinent and far beyond it. Buddhist monks
      from China - such as Xuanzang in the seventh century - learn Sanskrit specifically
      to read the sutras in the original and bring translations home. In monasteries
      from Nalanda to Luoyang a knowledge of Sanskrit means access to a thousand-year
      library of philosophy, medicine, mathematics, and astronomy. For a merchant
      trading with Indian monasteries or Brahmin communities, even a basic knowledge
      of Sanskrit greeting formulas is a mark of respect and trustworthiness.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753545
    m_Localized: Persian
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753546
    m_Localized: After the Arab conquest of 651 Middle Persian (Pahlavi) gradually
      yields to New Persian (Farsi) - a language now written in the Arabic alphabet
      but retaining Iranian grammar and a vast native vocabulary. Under the Abbasids,
      Persian officials and scholars hold key positions at court; Farsi becomes the
      language of poetry and diplomacy in the eastern caliphate. Zoroastrian communities
      of Yazd and Nishapur still write liturgical texts in Pahlavi. Along trade routes
      from Merv to Nishapur and onward into India, Farsi is understood in most bazaars.
      A knowledge of Persian allows a merchant to communicate with educated caliphate
      officials, read contracts, and navigate the Persio-Arabic commercial world.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753547
    m_Localized: Arabic
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753548
    m_Localized: 'Arabic emerged from the Arabian Peninsula with the Islamic armies
      of the seventh century and by the Tang era had become the language of administration,
      law, and worship from Iberia to Khurasan. Arabic script - a cursive ligature
      read right to left - is used to record the Quran and is carried over into administrative
      documents of the conquered peoples. In Baghdad and Damascus Arabic is simultaneously
      the language of markets, courts, and scholarly disputation. Quranic Arabic
      carries a special religious standing: Muslims of any origin strive to learn
      at least the suras used in prayer, creating a broad audience of comprehenders.
      For a merchant in the caliphate a working knowledge of Arabic cuts the cost
      of an interpreter and earns the trust of Muslim partners.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753549
    m_Localized: Greek
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11185858546753550
    m_Localized: Greek is the state language of the Byzantine Empire and the language
      of Orthodox worship; the Greek alphabet is used for secular documents and patristic
      texts alike. In Constantinople an educated merchant reads Plato and a trade
      contract with the same alphabet. Greek also served as the scholarly language
      of the Hellenistic world - through its mediation, Arab translators in Baghdad
      work from Aristotle and Galen. In Black Sea and Mediterranean ports a knowledge
      of Greek eases trade with Byzantine trading posts. Outside Constantinople and
      Asia Minor native speakers are fewer, but Greek retains the prestige of classical
      and Christian learning.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
